• SS.8.5.6.5 Investigate the role of political parties, including their influence on the electoral process and the shaping of public policy. • SS.8.5.6.6 Examine the structure and functions of the U.S. Congress in making laws and representing constituents. • SS.8.5.6.7 Analyze the role of the president in the executive branch, including their powers, responsibilities, and interactions with other branches of government. • SS.8.5.6.8 Examine the structure and function of the judicial branch in interpreting the meaning of laws.
Civic and Political
Institutions and Systems
Engaging in Civic Participation
Civics Anchor Standard 10
• SS.8.5.10.1 Explore the rights and responsibilities of people in a representative democracy. • SS.8.5.10.2 Analyze the significance of civic participation through activities such as voting, community service, and advocacy. • SS.8.5.10.3 Investigate the role of media and technology in shaping public opinion and facilitating civic engagement. • SS.8.5.10.4 Reflect on the responsibilities of active citizenship and the potential for individual and collective impact.
Rights, Roles, and
Responsibilities of Citizens
Examining Democratic Processes
Civics Anchor Standard 8
• SS.8.5.8.1 Investigate the election process and its impact on representation. • SS.8.5.8.2 Evaluate the importance of compromise, negotiation, and the rule of law in the functioning of democratic institutions.
Processes, Rules, and Laws
Civics Anchor Standard 7
• SS.8.5.7.1 Analyze the role of interest groups and lobbyists in influencing policy decisions and the democratic process. • SS.8.5.7.2 Evaluate the impact of public policies on various groups within society.
Participation and
Deliberation
Theme 6: Manifest Destiny and Indian Removal
Time Period: 1815 CE–1860 CE
Analyzing Multiple Perspectives
Inquiry Anchor Standard 2
• SS.8.6.2.1 Examine primary sources to understand the viewpoints on Manifest Destiny. • SS.8.6.2.2 Compare and contrast the perspectives on issues related to land ownership and sovereignty.
Evaluate Sources and
Evidence
• SS.8.6.21.1 Evaluate how different groups viewed westward expansion and the policies of Indian Removal.
Perspectives
• SS.8.6.23.1 Investigate the economic, social, and political factors that contributed to the idea of Manifest Destiny and westward expansion. • SS.8.6.23.2 Analyze the impact of technological advancements on the ability to settle in and transport goods to the western frontier.
Change, Continuity, and
Context
Civics Anchor Standard 8
• SS.8.6.8.1 Examine the conflicting interests between Native American tribes and the U.S. government, as well as the legal and political frameworks that shaped Indian Removal policies.
Cause and Effect
Civics Anchor Standard 8
• SS.8.6.8.2 Identify key events and policies related to westward expansion.
• SS.8.6.24.1 Examine the historical and ongoing consequences of Indian Removal on Native American communities, their cultures, and their relationships with the U.S. government.
Historical Thinking
Geography Anchor
• SS.8.6.16.1 Evaluate the impact of westward expansion on different regions and its contribution to the Civil War.
Standard 16
Human Environment
and Culture
Ethical Decision‐ Making
• SS.8.6.21.2 Investigate the historical and contemporary impacts of westward expansion on people and the environment. • SS.8.6.21.3 Investigate the role of individuals and groups in shaping responses to Indian Removal policies.
Perspectives
Inquiry Anchor Standard 5
• SS.8.6.5.1 Reflect on lessons from history to engage in discussions about the impact of history on modern society.
Informed Civic Discourse
and Engagement
Theme 7: The Civil War and Reform Movements
Time Period: 1837 CE–1877 CE
Analyzing Primary and Secondary Sources
Inquiry Anchor Standard 2
• SS.8.7.2.1 Identify and analyze primary sources to gain insights into the experiences of individuals during this era. • SS.8.7.2.2 Examine secondary sources to understand how interpretations of events differ.
Evaluate Sources and
Evidence
• SS.8.7.21.1 Analyze viewpoints from different time periods to evaluate changing perspectives.
Perspectives
• SS.8.7.21.2 Investigate the economic, political, and social factors that contributed to the sectional tensions leading to secession and the outbreak of the Civil War.
Perspectives
• SS.8.7.4.1 Examine slavery as a central issue in the Civil War, its role in regional economies, and the arguments for its abolition.
Historical Thinking
• SS.8.7.23.1 Analyze the social and cultural factors that led to the rise of social and political reform movements.
Change, Continuity, and
Context
Cause and Effect
History Anchor Standard 22
• SS.8.7.22.1 Identify the key events of the Civil War and their significance in shaping the outcome of the conflict and the nation.
Historical Sources and
Evidence
• SS.8.7.23.2 Examine the social and political changes brought about by the Emancipation Proclamation, and the 13th, 14th, and 15th Amendments. • SS.8.7.23.3 Evaluate the impact of reform movements on historical and contemporary societies.
Change, Continuity, and
Context
• SS.8.7.23.4 Analyze the contributions of key individuals to the advancement of civil rights and social change. • SS.8.7.23.5 Investigate the role of social movements in shaping political and cultural landscapes.
Change, Continuity, and
Context
Inquiry Anchor Standard 5
• SS.8.7.5.1 Engage in collaborative discussions about the ongoing struggle for civil rights, equal justice, and the responsibilities of citizenship in a diverse society.
Informed Civic Discourse
and Engagement
Theme 8: Reconstruction
Time Period: 1865–1877
• SS.8.8.22.1 Identify and analyze primary sources to gain insights into the experiences of African Americans during Reconstruction.
Historical Sources and
Evidence
Inquiry Anchor Standard 2
• SS.8.8.2.1 Examine secondary sources to understand differing interpretations of the Reconstruction era.
Evaluate Sources and
Evidence
• SS.8.8.21.1 Compare and contrast viewpoints from different historical perspectives to evaluate changing attitudes toward race, citizenship, and civil rights.
Perspectives
• SS.8.8.22.2 Investigate the social, economic, and political conditions that led to Reconstruction.
Historical Sources and
Evidence
• SS.8.8.21.1 Examine the lingering effects of slavery on the lives of newly freed African Americans. • SS.8.8.21.2 Analyze the resistance to change and the rise of white supremacist ideologies as significant challenges during Reconstruction.
Perspectives
Cause and Effect
Civics Anchor Standard 8
• SS.8.8.8.1 Identify the key policies and legislation of Reconstruction, including the 13th, 14th, and 15th Amendments, and assess their significance in advancing civil rights. • SS.8.8.8.2 Evaluate the establishment of white supremacist organizations and the enforcement of Jim Crow laws.
Ethical Decision‐ Making
• SS.8.8.24.1 Investigate the historical and contemporary impact of Reconstruction.
Historical Thinking
Civics Anchor Standard 7
• SS.8.8.7.1 Reflect on lessons from history to engage in discussions about the ongoing struggle to promote social change in modern society.
Participation and
