Sequencing
n Systematically sequencing texts and tasks so that they build upon one another n Continuing to model close/analytical reading of complex texts during teacher readalouds while also ensuring students build proficiency in reading complex texts themselves
n Focusing on the language demands of texts, particularly those that may be especially difficult for ELs n Carefully sequencing tasks to build understanding and effective use of the language in texts
Rereading
n Rereading the text or selected passages to look for answers to questions or to clarify points of confusion
n Rereading the text to build understanding of ideas and language incrementally (e.g., beginning with literal comprehension questions on initial readings and moving to inferential and analytical comprehension questions on subsequent reads) n Repeated exposure to rich language over time, focusing on particular language (e.g., different vocabulary) during each reading
Instructional Strategies
Strategies
Tools
n Teaching students to develop outlines, charts, diagrams, graphic organizers, or other tools to summarize and synthesize content n Teaching students to annotate text (mark text and make notes) for specific elements (e.g., confusing vocabulary, main ideas, evidence)
n Explicitly modeling how to use the outlines or graphic organizers to analyze/discuss a model text and providing guided practice for students before they use the tools independently n Using the tools as a scaffold for discussions or writing
Writing
n Teaching students to return to the text as they write in response to the text and providing them with models and feedback
n Providing opportunities for students to talk about their ideas with a peer before (or after) writing n Providing written language models (e.g., charts of important words or powerful sentences) n Providing reference frames (e.g., sentence, paragraph, and text organization frames), as appropriate
Source: English Language Arts/English Language Development Framework for California Public Schools (ELA/ELD Framework), pp. 75–76.
Instructional Strategies
To become broadly literate, students need to read regularly and frequently as a part of classroom instruction. High-quality instructional materials for each content area should provide appropriate reading selections. In addition, teachers and teacher librarians work together to develop classroom and library collections of books that support all content areas and genres—literary and informational. See figure 21.2 for the range of text types that students should experience.
