and Context","Sample Compelling
• What is the purpose of government? • What is the balance between the rights and responsibilities of citizenship?
Questions
• Postwar Challenges: Articles of Confederation, debt crisis, Shays’ Rebellion, state militias • Compromises: commerce clause, Great Compromise, Three-Fifths Compromise
• Structure: executive, judicial, and legislative branches, federalism • Powers: appropriations, checks and balances, declarations of war, impeachment, judicial review, legislation, veto
• Types of Liberties: civil liberties, criminal law protections, political freedoms, religious freedom
Sample Compelling
• What does it mean to be a good citizen? • When is restricting the right to vote justified?
Questions
• Citizenship: 1790 Alien Naturalization Act, Native Americans • Voting Rights: free Blacks, Jacksonian Democracy, property qualifications, women
• Factions to Parties: Anti-Federalists, Democratic- Republicans, Federalists, Jacksonian Democrats, Whigs • Dividing Issues: state debts, Whiskey Rebellion, Alien and Sedition Acts, War of 1812, Panic of 1819, Second Bank of the United States
Sample Compelling
• Were the justifications for territorial expansion “just”? • How can we make amends for historical wrongs?
Questions
Therefore, the student is able to
• Ideological: Hudson River School, Manifest Destiny, Romanticism • Socioeconomic: agricultural expansion, canals, cotton production, fur trade, gold rush, Homestead Act, population growth, railroads, steamboats, technological advances
• Territorial Acquisition: Northwest Ordinance, Louisiana Purchase, Florida, Texas Annexation, Oregon, Mexican Cession, Gadsden Purchase, Alaska • Frontier Migration: Oregon Trail, Santa Fe Trail, Mormon Trail • International Wars: Indian Wars, Mexican-American War
• Confederacies and Alliances: Cherokee Confederacy, Ghost Dance, Tecumseh, • Revolts and Warfare: Battle of Fallen Timbers, Seminole Wars, Dakota War, Bear River Massacre, Great Plains Wars, Chief Joseph, Geronimo
• Depopulation and Impoverishment: buffalo eradication, disease, warfare • Displacement and Reservations: Trail of Tears, Oklahoma Territory, Long Walk of the Navajo • Forced Assimilation: missionaries, reservations, Indian boarding schools
Sample Compelling Questions
• Did industrialization improve daily life? • What sparks innovation?
• Work Life: clock time, factory system, putting-out system, slave labor, wage work • Society: emerging middle class, immigration, leisure, women’s sphere, urbanization
• Transportation: National Road, Erie Canal, steamboats, railroads • Communication: Pony Express, telegraph • Production: cotton gin, interchangeable parts, Lowell mills, steel plow
• Labor Organizations and Conflicts: cycles of joblessness, guilds, Lowell strikes, workingmen’s parties • Social Divisions: economic inequality, expansion of slavery
Sample Compelling
• How can oppressed people respond to injustice? • Why does forced labor exist?
Questions
• Types of slavery: artisans, gang labor system, plantations, servants, urban slavery • North: finance, textile production • South: Cotton Kingdom, internal slave trade
• Repression: illiteracy, paternalism, slave codes, violence • Adaptation: family, music, religion, slave community • Resistance: Amistad, deception, Gabriel’s Rebellion, Harriet Tubman, Nat Turner, sabotage, Underground Railroad
• What is a perfect society? • How can people make society better?
• Religion: Hiram Bingham, Methodism, Mormonism, revival meetings • Utopian Communities: New Harmony, Oneidas, Shakers
Abolitionism: American Anti-Slavery Society, Frederick Douglass, Harriet Beecher Stowe, William Lloyd Garrison • Women’s Rights: Elizabeth Cady Stanton, Seneca Falls Convention • Alcohol: Women’s Christian Temperance Union • Prisons and Asylums: Dorothea Dix • Public Education: Horace Mann
Sample Compelling
• Does war settle issues? • Did the North win the Civil War or did the South lose it?
Questions
• Antebellum Conflicts: states’ rights, Compromise of 1850, Dred Scott case, Bleeding Kansas, John Brown’s raids, Lincoln’s election, secession • Debates: free-labor ideology, Lincoln-Douglas campaign, pro-slavery arguments, Republican Party
• Social and Economic Factors: disease, manufacturing, national currency, population, slaves • Leadership: Abraham Lincoln, Emancipation Proclamation, Jefferson Davis, Robert E. Lee, Ulysses S. Grant • Military: Antietam, Fifty-Fourth Massachusetts Volunteers, Gettysburg, naval blockade, Sherman’s March
Sample Compelling
• Did Reconstruction fail? • How should societies reconcile after conflict?
Questions
• Federal Action: Sherman Field Order 15, Presidential Reconstruction, Freedmen’s Bureau, Radical Republicans, Civil Rights Act of 1875, Reconstruction Amendments • Freedpeople: Black churches, Black officeholders, Black schools and colleges, Juneteenth
• End of Reconstruction: Ku Klux Klan, Panic of 1873, racial terrorism, Slaughterhouse Cases, Compromise of 1877 • Restrictions of Freedom: Black Codes, convict leasing, Jim Crow, Plessy v. Ferguson, sharecropping",,HCSSSgr8USHist_d361b9.pdf,"HCSSSgr8USHist_d361b9.pdf: Inquiry standards table (u2-u32) is headed 'Grades 6-8 Inquiry Standards': labeled with that band, course null.
