1.​ Investors in financial assets expect an increase in value over time (capital gain) and/or receipt of regular income, such as interest or dividends. 2.​ Common types of financial assets include certificates of deposit (CDs), stocks, bonds, mutual funds, and real estate. 3.​ Instead of buying individual stocks and bonds, investors can buy shares of pooled investments such as mutual funds and exchange-traded funds (ETFs). 4.​ Different types of investments expose investors to different degrees of risk. 5.​ A person’s investment risk tolerance depends on factors such as personality, financial resources, investment experiences, and life circumstances.
