• Spatial organization of the economic, transportation, and
communication systems
• Connections with support networks of trade in raw materials,
manufactured goods, capital (human and monetary), ideas,
and services
• Impacts of changes, distribution, and importance of resources
Iowa's industrial and economic development is directly tied to agriculture, biosciences, machinery, and renewable energies. Iowa’s unique geographic position on the Mississippi River contributes to its economic development and connection with global trade networks.","9th-12th Grade
