Culture can be defined as the main characteristics shared by a group of people at a particular time and in a particular place that are learned and passed down by rules, traditions and stories. Culture can include, but is not limited to, characteristics like religions, philosophies, moral codes, language, social relations, technologies, institutions and organizations. The culture of a community is unique and is illustrated by the traditions found in that community. These traditions may be passed on informally from person to person and can be described through song, story, decoration, ritual and/or celebration. Due to Kentucky’s varied landscape, community culture is often built around features specific to an area, which can include, but is not limited to, farming, crafts or industry. Students can describe how culture and experience impact the community. They can identify how the culture of the community contributes to the health of the community.
