7. Explain scarcity and discuss how it is experienced by individuals, governments, and societies. 8. Explain that making good choices should involve trading off the expected value of one opportunity against the expected value of its best alternative. 9. Discuss how the choices people make have both present and future consequences and differ by individuals and societies. 10. Explain how choices are constrained by available resources. 11. Discuss ways that decisions made by individuals, firms, or government officials often have unintended consequences that can, partially or entirely, offset or supplement the initial effects of the decision. 12. Relate marginal benefit and marginal cost to choice. 13. Evaluate the role that risk takes in decision making and that risk can be reduced by diversification.
E.2 Evaluate different economic systems.
6. Explain that scarcity requires the use of some distribution method to allocate goods, services, and resources, whether the method is selected explicitly or not. 7. Contrast a market economy from a command economy. 8. Analyze the three major economic questions: What goods and services will be produced? How will these goods and services be produced? Who will consume them? 9. Describe how various economic systems rely on government directives (central planning) and signals (prices) from private markets to allocate scarce goods, services, and productive resources. 10. Compare the benefits and costs of different allocation methods.
Standard
Objectives
E.3 Examine how voluntary exchanges and trade are reflections of positive and negative incentives.
12. Describe how consumers, producers, workers, savers, investors, and citizens respond to incentives to allocate their scarce resources in ways that provide them the highest possible net benefits. 13. Explain how free trade increases the worldwide material standard of living. 14. Identify gains from free trade and recognize they are not distributed equally, and some individuals or groups may lose more than they gain when trade barriers are reduced. 15. Explain why many nations employ trade barriers to restrict free trade for national defense reasons, to protect key industries, or because some companies and workers are hurt by free trade. 16. Explain why import restrictions by public policies result in higher prices and decreased job opportunities and profits. 17. Define labor productivity. 18. Evaluate how international economic interdependence causes economic conditions and policies in one nation to be affected by economic conditions and policies in other nations. 19. Describe the comparative advantage in the production of goods or services when a product is produced at a lower opportunity cost than other individuals or nations. 20. Demonstrate that international trade stems mainly from factors that confer comparative advantage, including international differences in the availability of productive resources and differences in relative prices. 21. Define transaction cost and explain why trade increases with transition cost decreases. 22. Illustrate how goods can be produced at lowest opportunity cost depending on many factors, including available resources, technology, and political and economic institutions.
