8. Examine the functions and relationships among the three branches of government. 9. Identify the organization and jurisdiction of federal, state, and local courts and their interrelationships. 10. Assess the scope of presidential power and decision making through examination of legislation and military action. 11. Describe the organization, jurisdiction, and proceedings of federal courts. 12. Evaluate how John Marshall established the Supreme Court as an independent, co-equal branch of government through his opinion in Marbury v. Madison (1803). 13. Compare the philosophies of judicial activism and judicial restraint and explain their role in Supreme Court decision making.
USG.4 Analyze the creation and implementation of public policy in the United States.
14. Examine how the national government influences the public agenda and shapes public policy. 15. Describe the process by which public policy is formed and implemented by the national, state, and local government. 16. Compare the processes of lawmaking by national, state, and local governments. 17. Analyze how individuals, interest groups, lobbyists, and the media influence public policy. 18. Evaluate how the judiciary influences public policy by delineating the power of government and safeguarding the rights of the individual.
Standard
Objectives
USG.5 Analyze of the role of federalism in addressing the distribution of power between the national, state, and local governments.
8. Explain the relationship and powers shared between state governments and the national government. 9. Trace the extent to which power is shared by all levels of government. 10. Examine the powers denied to state governments and national government. 11. Evaluate the balance of power between state governments and national government related to funding. 12. Investigate how the amendment process protects both the national government and state governments. 13. Identify the major responsibilities and sources of revenue for state and local governments. 14. Analyze the various interpretations and extent of the federal government’s power provided by the Ninth and Tenth Amendments.
Standard
Objectives
USG.6 Differentiate civil rights from civil liberties and describe how each have been interpreted and amended throughout United States’ history.
8. Examine the civil liberties and rights guaranteed in the Bill of Rights. 9. Explain due process of law as expressed in the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments. 10. Evaluate the balance between individual liberties and the public order. 11. Analyze changing interpretations of the Bill of Rights over time, particularly the First and Fourteenth Amendments. 12. Analyze judicial activism and restraint as well as the effects of each policy over the decades. 13. Evaluate the effects of the Court’s interpretations of the Constitution in Marbury v. Madison (1803), McCulloch v. Maryland (1819), and United States v. Nixon (1974). 14. Investigate the controversies that have resulted over changing interpretations of civil rights.
Standard
Objectives
USG.7 Describe the role and function of linkage institutions such as the media, interest groups, political parties, and political action committees, on the citizens and federal government.
8. Describe the controversies over campaign funding. 9. Evaluate the decision Citizens United v. Federal Election Commission (2010) on campaign financing. 10. Examine how linkage groups impact primary and general elections as well as citizen involvement in campaigns. 11. Identify major interest groups and their major agenda messages. 12. Evaluate the responsibility of citizens to thoughtfully examine information presented by media and interest groups in forming individual political opinions. 13. Identify the role of journalism in the political process and trace its development over time. 14. Examine the role and history of Political Action Committees and interest groups on the electoral process.
Standard
Objectives
USG.8 Describe and evaluate the role, rights, and responsibility of a citizen in the American democracy.
12. Evaluate the effectiveness of citizen efforts to influence decisions of state and local governments by examining events. 13. Compare the ways that citizens participate in the political process: voting, campaigning, lobbying, filing a legal challenge, demonstrating, petitioning, picketing, and running for political office. 14. Analyze trends in voter turnout. 15. Investigate the causes and effects of reapportionment and redistricting, including spatial districting and the rights of minorities. 16. Examine the function of the Electoral College. 17. Identify the importance of each of the rights guaranteed by the Bill of Rights and how each is secured, specifically freedom of religion, speech, press, assembly, petition, privacy. 18. Identify the importance of economic rights and explain how they are secured. 19. Discuss the legal obligations to obey the law, serve as a juror, and pay taxes. 20. Justify the obligations of civic mindedness: voting, being informed on civic issues, volunteering, performing public service, and serving in the military or alternative service. 21. Explain reciprocity between rights and obligations. 22. Describe how one becomes a citizen of the United States.
