11. Analyze the impact of radio, cinema, and print media on the creation of mass culture. 12. Analyze works of major American artists and writers, including F. Scott Fitzgerald, Ernest Hemingway, and H.L. Mencken, to characterize the era of the 1920s. 13. Determine the relationship between technological innovations and the creation of increased leisure time. 14. Assess effects of overproduction, stock market speculation, and restrictive monetary policies on the pending economic crisis. 15. Compare and contrast the impact of the Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act on the global economy and the resulting worldwide depression. 16. Analyze the impact of the changes in the 1920s on the economy, society, and culture, including mass production, the role of credit, the effect of radio in creating a mass culture, and the cultural changes exemplified by the Great Migration and the Harlem Renaissance. 17. Debate the causes and effects of the social change and conflict between traditional and modern culture that took place during the 1920s, including the role of women, the Red Scare, immigration quotas, Prohibition, and the Scopes trial. 18. Examine notable authors of the 1920s, including John Steinbeck, William Faulkner, Zora Neale Hurston, and Langston Hughes. 19. Analyze the Great Depression for its impact on the American family, including the Bonus Army, Hoovervilles, Dust Bowl, and Dorothea Lange. 20. Investigate conditions created by the Dust Bowl for their impact on migration patterns during the Great Depression.
