[Psychology] 7. Interpret the major elements of classical conditioning including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli, conditioned and unconditioned responses, neutral stimuli, acquisition, and stimulus generalization. 8. Explain the development of operant conditioning and evaluate the usefulness of reward versus punishment as it relates to learning. 9. Examine cognitive and observational learning processes.
[Psychology] PSY.7 Combine the elements of memory and concept formation to examine how those cognitive processes contribute to perception and judgment. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 5. Diagram the stages of memory formation including the processes of sensory memory, working memory, and long-term memory. 6. Distinguish between concepts, concept hierarchies, schemas, and prototypes.
[Psychology] PSY.8 Distinguish the elements that give rise to emotional experience, explain the various theories of emotion, and summarize the significance of emotional intelligence on mental wellness. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 7. Explain the universal nature of emotions and emotional expression including the work of Paul Ekman and Robert Plutchik and the concept of display rules. 8. Compare and contrast theories of emotion, including: James-Lange, Canon-Bard, Shachter-Singer (Two Factor), Cognitive Appraisal, and Opponent Process theories. 9. Discuss emotional intelligence and its impact on mental wellness and interpersonal relationships.
[Psychology] PSY.9 Discuss the role of biological drives and motivations on human behavior and cognitive processes. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 4. Distinguish between intrinsic and extrinsic sources of motivation. 5. Describe the concept of need for achievement and its significance in understanding motivational differences among people. 6. Employ Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs to understand human behavioral priorities.
[Psychology] PSY.10 Breakdown the various theories of human cognitive and social development. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 6. Recall biological concepts related to human development including genetic structure, prenatal development, and infancy. 7. Trace the physical development and the development of motor skills through early childhood. 8. Diagram Piaget’s Stages of Cognitive Development and describe the characteristics of each stage. 9. Compare and contrast cognitive and social perspectives of an individual through Erikson’s Psychosocial Stages of Development. 10. Examine theories of moral development including Kohlberg’s Stages of Moral Reasoning.
[Psychology] PSY.11 Outline the major personality theories, assessments, and practices of type and trait theorists and justify the practical applications of their work. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 5. Compare and contrast Freudian and Neo-Freudian perspectives on personality, including: the Freudian model of the mind, the unconscious mind, psychosexual development, archetypes, neuroticism, basic anxiety, and the inferiority complex. 6. Explain behavioral, social-cognitive, and humanistic concepts related to personality including reciprocal determinism, locus of control, unconditional positive regard, and the fully functioning person. 7. Describe the five-factor model of personality. 8. Explain methods used by researchers to assess personality, including: projective tests and personality inventories.
[Psychology] PSY.12 Outline the historical progression of intelligence research and explain the major theories of intelligence including the work of Charles Spearman, Robert Sternberg, and Howard Gardner. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 4. Appraise theories of intelligence including Spearman’s g factor, Sternberg’s triarchic theory, and Gardner’s theory of multiple intelligences. 5. Describe historical and contemporary tools for measuring intelligence, including: the intelligence quotient and the roles they play in categorizing intellectual ability (normal range, intellectual disability, giftedness). 6. Examine the history of cultural bias in intelligence measures and the sociocultural underpinnings of those processes.
[Psychology] PSY.13 Formulate the conditions that lead to mental wellness, including a positive self-concept, healthy cognitive processes, and edifying interpersonal relationships. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 4. Examine the role of the stress response and its contribution to physical and psychological health. 5. Describe the field of positive psychology and its application of psychological concepts to improve mental well-being. 6. Appraise the role of sleep, self-concept, need for achievement, and interpersonal relationships in establishing healthy cognitive and emotional processes.
[Psychology] PSY.14 Describe major psychological disorders, their symptoms, and classifications. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 3. Differentiate the different types of mental illness including mood disorders, dissociative disorders, personality disorders, anxiety disorders, somatoform disorders, and schizophrenia. 4. Employ the DSM-V as a tool for diagnosing mental disorders as illustrated in case studies.
[Psychology] PSY.15 Explain the methods used by mental health professionals to treat people living with mental illness. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 5. Distinguish between the work of a psychiatrist and psychologist in treating mental illness. 6. Examine the role of stigma in preventing people from accessing adequate mental healthcare. 7. Compare and contrast psychodynamic, humanistic, and behavioral therapies in the treatment of mental disorders. 8. Describe the usefulness of cognitive therapy, cognitive-behavioral therapy, and group therapies in the treatment of mental disorders.
[Psychology] PSY.16 Describe the influence of social factors on individual cognition and behavior including conformity, obedience, the bystander effect, bias, and polarization. (Strands: Civics, Civil Rights, History)
[Psychology] 5. Describe the concept of social role and conformity as illustrated in the Stanford Prison
[Psychology] Experiment and other studies.
[Psychology] 6. Explain the findings of research into obedience and conformity as illustrated in the work
[Psychology] of Stanley Milgram and Solomon Asch.
[Psychology] 7. Examine the concepts of bias, polarization, and the bystander effect.
[Psychology] 8. Identify the major elements of behavioral economics as evidenced by the research of
[Psychology] Amos Tversky, Daniel Kahneman, and Dan Ariely.
