Learning Assessment Objectives: Students demonstrate an ability to: a. Identify physical locations of the communities being looked at and their locations on a map, explain what the physical environment is like for the communities being looked at, and analyze the ways environment influences their ways of life b. Identify what resources, goods, and services are available to the communities being looked at, and explain ways they obtain those resources and the influences of environment on resource availability c. Analyze the similarities and differences of the communities being looked at and those of the students (e.g., ways of life, traditions, celebrations, the way communities are organized spatially, community leaders, language, currency)","Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is a community? ● What does it mean to be a member of a community? ● What are important places in a community? ● What communities do students belong to?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are the relative locations of home and school? ● What are students’ home addresses? ● What are the addresses of family members and close family friends? ● Why are directions useful?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is the landscape of the local community? ● What buildings are in the local community? ● What type of homes are in the local community? ● How do resources, goods, and services support the people in the local community?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What natural resources are in the local community? ● How does the local community use available natural resources? ● How do natural resources affect jobs?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is culture and diversity? ● What cultures are represented in local communities? ● How are local cultures similar and different? ● How do people show pride in their cultures? ● Why is it important to respect different cultures?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● How does migration change communities? ● What factors lead people to move to the local community? ● What factors lead people to move out of the local community? ● What are human-environmental factors that change communities?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What goods are available in the community? ● What services are available in the community? ● What are some needs of the local community? ● How do goods and services fulfill community needs?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are jobs and what are careers? ● Why do people need jobs? ● What jobs are prevalent in the community? ● Why do people have different jobs and careers and how do they contribute to the community? ● How are wants and needs fulfilled through earning an income?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is money? ● Why do people need money? ● How can people save money and why is that important? ● How do limitations set by scarcity shape people’s choices? ● What is abundance?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is citizenship and what are the responsibilities that come with citizenship? ● What are human rights and civil rights? ● How can students serve within the community? ● Why is it important for people to participate in creating rules and laws? ● What does it mean for things to be equal and/or fair?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are characteristics of an effective leader? ● What are different types of leaders? (e.g., community organizer, social justice leader, elected officials like the mayor) ● How do community members elect or choose a leader? ● What are the responsibilities of a leader? ● How do leaders make decisions?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are school rules and responsibilities? ● What are family rules and responsibilities? ● What are community rules and responsibilities? ● How and why do rules change depending on the setting?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● How can leaders resolve problems when people do not agree with each other? ● How can people discuss and respect each other's viewpoints when disagreeing? ● How can we resolve problems at home and school?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is social change? ● How does societal change happen? ● Who are people who have created change in society?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What communities are near the local community? ● What are the geographical characteristics of those communities? ● What are resources within those communities? ● How are the communities similar and different from one another and from the students’ communities?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are the characteristics of communities in different parts of the United States? ● What are the similarities and differences between communities in other places and the local community? (It is suggested that teachers use students’ backgrounds and/or student choice to pick additional communities to study)
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are communities around the globe like? ● What is their geography like? ● What are the resources of the community? ● What language(s) do the people of those communities speak? ● What is their money called? ● Who are their community leaders? (It is suggested that teachers use students’ backgrounds and/or student choice to pick example areas to look at. These should be different from those looked at in kindergarten)",,RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf,"RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: Guide/doc check: guide row clusters K,1-8,9-12 and topics match; this part (u1-u3456) covers K through Grade 7 through SS7.6.1 only. u3456 is a furniture unit (running header) and is not labeled; last labeled unit is u3455 (end of SS7.6.1 table); SS7.6.2 starts at u3457 (next part).
RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: u68-u93: Anchor Standards overview, the four-verb rigor hierarchy definitions, CRSE design principles and footnotes labeled backup (explanatory); u70-u74 are an extracted diagram of the four domains with fragmentary text ('Rhode Island's Social St' / 'udies Anchor Standards'). u95-u113 anchor standards (4 levels per anchor, one unit per domain/anchor group, merged text) labeled standards with grades K-12 since they apply to all grades.
RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: u133, u478, u866, u1204, u1840, u2317, u2782, u3196: grade title + narrative description in one unit; labeled standards because title and narrative are merged (narrative would otherwise be examples). u478 and u866 also merge the Topics-of-Inquiry list (standard codes+titles) into the same unit.
