Learning Assessment Objectives: Students demonstrate an ability to: a. Identify historical figures who brought positive social change (e.g., Mahatma Gandhi, Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr., Mother Teresa, Nelson Mandela, Susan B Anthony, Jane Goodall), and explain how they impacted change throughout the world and the challenges they faced b. Identify contemporary individuals and groups who are working on positive social change (e.g., Greta Thunberg, scientists who work on vaccines, people working for human rights, and front-line workers such as firefighters, police, teachers, doctors, and nurses) and explain how they impact change and the challenges they face c. Explain ways that students can contribute to positive change on small and large scales (e.g., in their schools, community, country, or world)","Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are map and globe terms and concepts? ● What is the difference between a relative and absolute location? ● How are different types of maps and globes used?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● Where are the seven continents and five oceans? ● Where are mountains and why are they important? ● Where are deserts and why are they important? ● What are different types of bodies of water and how are they used?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are natural resources and where are they available around the globe? ● How are these resources important for plant and animal life, as well as humans? ● How do people obtain and use these resources? ● How does the presence or absence of natural resources affect human settlement patterns?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What can different environments provide to humans and how does that affect human settlement? ● What are natural disasters and how can they affect human settlement and economics? ● How have human-environmental interactions and natural disasters influenced migration? ● How has the climate changed over the years?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are the characteristics of culture? ● How are global cultures similar and different? ● What does it mean to be Indigenous and who are the Indigenous groups around the world? ● Why is it important to respect different cultures?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● In what ways can human activities affect the environment? ● What is pollution and how does it affect the world? ● How do human activities affect animals’ natural environments? ● How do changes in the environment affect human populations? ● In what ways can humans help the environment?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● Why do people migrate? ● What are the differences between voluntary and forced migration? ● What events and other factors lead people to migrate? ● What effects can migration have on environments or other peoples?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is cultural heritage? ● What cultural heritages are represented by the students in the classroom and by members of local communities? ● How do we celebrate global cultures in the local community?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are imports and exports? ● How do imports and exports create a global economy? ● How do we as citizens contribute to a global economy?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● How do people communicate around the world? ● How has the internet changed global communications? ● Why do people need to communicate globally?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What is global citizenship? ● What are ways we can engage in global citizenship? ● How can we as humans resolve global problems together?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What are the different types of governments in the world? ● Who are the leaders of those governments? ● What are the characteristics of a global leader? ● Who are global leaders who are not government leaders?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● Why do countries use diplomacy to work together? ● How are global citizens responsible to each other?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● What can happen when countries don’t work together? ● What is war and what are the major causes of war? ● What are ways that governments resolve conflicts? ● What is peace?
Guiding Questions for Instruction: ● Who are people who have brought social change to the world and what was their impact? ● Do you have to be a leader in government to bring positive social change? ● In what ways can we bring change to the local community and world around us?",,RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf,"RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: Guide/doc check: guide row clusters K,1-8,9-12 and topics match; this part (u1-u3456) covers K through Grade 7 through SS7.6.1 only. u3456 is a furniture unit (running header) and is not labeled; last labeled unit is u3455 (end of SS7.6.1 table); SS7.6.2 starts at u3457 (next part).
RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: u68-u93: Anchor Standards overview, the four-verb rigor hierarchy definitions, CRSE design principles and footnotes labeled backup (explanatory); u70-u74 are an extracted diagram of the four domains with fragmentary text ('Rhode Island's Social St' / 'udies Anchor Standards'). u95-u113 anchor standards (4 levels per anchor, one unit per domain/anchor group, merged text) labeled standards with grades K-12 since they apply to all grades.
RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: u133, u478, u866, u1204, u1840, u2317, u2782, u3196: grade title + narrative description in one unit; labeled standards because title and narrative are merged (narrative would otherwise be examples). u478 and u866 also merge the Topics-of-Inquiry list (standard codes+titles) into the same unit.
RhodeIsland_SocialStudiesStandards_FULL_4c95f6.pdf: Topic overview lists (u134, u480, u867, u1205, u1207, u1841, u1843, u2318, u2320, u2783, u2785, u3197, u3199) list inquiry topics, compelling questions and standard codes/titles; labeled standards. They contain the compelling questions (normally examples) merged with the standard titles. Topic list continues across pages (e.g., u1207, u1843, u2320, u2785, u3199).
