Number",TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf,"TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Social Studies Practices (SSP.01-SSP.06) are repeated verbatim in every grade/course section. Each in-section copy is labeled with that section's grade (K, 1, 2, 3, ... 8) or HS course (grades 9-12 with course set); the master Table 5 (u194-u241) is additionally labeled standards with band ranges K-2, 3-5, 6-8, 9-12 (code cells K-12). Assembler may see duplicate SSP text in a grade row because of both Table 5 and the section copy.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Topic units of the form 'Topic title Overview: ...' merge a topic heading with its overview narrative; because units are not split they are labeled standards (heading + overview). Units that begin with 'Overview:' (heading is a separate unit) are labeled examples (topic overview narrative) and the separate heading unit is standards. Impact: overview narrative text sits in standards for most topics.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Grade titles merged with course description in one unit (u361, u455, u560, u721, u930, u1081, u1147, u1746, u2326, u3091) are labeled standards (title + description mixed); the description-only units (starting 'Course Description:') are examples.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Table 1 (u61-u89) and Table 2 (u95-u127) course/grade descriptions in the front matter labeled examples (descriptive narrative, duplicating section course descriptions); Table 1 grade 5 rows u76-u80 cover Part 1 and Part 2, both grade 5.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Grades 6, 7, 8 titles are 'World History and Geography...' / 'United States History and Geography...' but are grade-level content: course null.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: HS course strings follow Table 2 titles with credit/asterisk notes removed: Economics, United States Government and Civics, United States History and Geography: Post-Reconstruction to the Present, World History and Geography: The Industrial Revolution to the Contemporary World. Table 2 lists the civics course code as 'GC' and the sections also use GC.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Table column header cells (Standard, Content Standard, Content Strand, Number, Practice, Social Studies Practice, Standard Number) and the repeated strand-key page footer ('C-Culture, E-Economics, ...') are labeled backup in the surrounding grade/course.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: u2029 (8.63): standard text split across table cells u2027 and u2029 ('...decision to' / 'secede.'); both standards.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: u2021, u2025, u2028, u2032, u2035, u2038, u2041 (8.62-8.67): strand cell appears in column C7 instead of C5; table layout shifted, grade 8 Civil War topic.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: Multi-column bullet lists are flattened in interleaved order in some standards (e.g. u1831 8.16, u4023 US.50, u4133 US.77, u4166 US.84, u4186 US.89, u4587 W.39, u4637 W.52), so bullet items appear out of reading order; each kept as a single standards unit.
TN_Academic_Social_Studies_Standards_25_0162ee.pdf: State guide check: guide says grade groupings [0]..[9-12] with 'Grade 5 split into two parts' - confirmed; electives and courses are all 9-12; guide mentions Geography/History/Civics topics - confirmed. Psychology, Sociology, African American History, Contemporary Issues, Ancient History, Economics, Tennessee History, US Government and Civics, US History and Geography, World Geography, World History and Geography are the 11 HS courses. Appendix A (CCSSO graphic, intro) is referenced but not present in the text."
TN_09-12,TN,"[9,10,11,12]",,,,"Collect data and information from a variety of primary and secondary sources, including: ● Printed materials ● Graphic representations ● Field observations/ Landscape analysis ● Artifacts ● Media and technology sources ● Oral History
SSP.01
Critically examine a primary or secondary source in order to: ● Extract, summarize, and paraphrase significant ideas ● Discern differences between evidence and assertion ● Recognize the significance of author’s purpose, point of view, and bias ● Draw logical inferences and conclusions ● Assess the strengths and limitations of arguments
SSP.02
Synthesize data from a variety of sources in order to: ● Establish accuracy and validity by comparing sources to each other ● Recognize disparities among multiple accounts ● Frame appropriate questions for further investigation
SSP.03
Construct and communicate arguments by citing supporting evidence to: ● Demonstrate and defend an understanding of ideas ● Compare and contrast viewpoints ● Illustrate cause and effect ● Predict likely outcomes ● Devise new outcomes or solutions ● Engage in appropriate civic discourse
SSP.04
Develop historical awareness by: ● Recognizing how and why historical accounts change over time ● Perceiving and presenting past events and issues as they might have been experienced by the people of the time, with historical empathy vs. present- mindedness ● Evaluating how unique circumstances of time and place create context and contribute to action and reaction ● Identifying patterns of continuity and change over time, making connections to the present
SSP.05
Develop geographic awareness by: ● Analyzing and determining the use of diverse types of maps based on the origin, authority, structure, context, and validity ● Using the geographic perspective to analyze relationships, patterns, and diffusion across space at multiple scales ● Analyzing locations, conditions, and connections of places and using maps to investigate spatial associations among phenomena ● Examining how geographers use regions and how perceptions of regions are fluid across time and space ● Analyzing interaction between humans and the physical environment
