Inquiry Process (K-12): Students act as social scientists as they ask questions, think critically, solve problems, and share conclusions.
C3 Indicators (6-8):
Constructing Compelling and Supporting Questions: D1.1, 2, 3; His. 12
Gathering and Evaluating Sources: D2. His. 6, 10, 12, 13; D3. 1, 2, 3
Communicating and Critiquing Conclusions: D2. His. 16; D4. 1, 2, 4
Overarching Themes:
• Foundations and Roles • Economics and Policy • Geography (in grades 6 and 7) • Civic Participation in a Democracy
Civics
Theme: Foundations and Roles
Civic and Political Institutions
D2.Civ.1.6-8. Distinguish the powers and responsibilities of citizens, political parties, interest groups, and the media in a variety of governmental and nongovernmental contexts.
D2.Civ.2.6-8. Explain specific roles played by citizens (such as voters, jurors, taxpayers, members of the armed forces, petitioners, protesters, and office-holders).
D2.Civ.3.6-8. Examine the origins, purposes, and impact of constitutions, laws, treaties, and international agreements.
D2.Civ.4.6-8. Explain the powers and limits of the three branches of government, public officials, and bureaucracies at different levels in the United States and in other countries.
D2.Civ.5.6-8. Explain the origins, functions, and structure of government with reference to the U.S. Constitution, state constitutions, and selected other systems of government.
D2.Civ.6.6-8. Describe the roles of political, civil, and economic organizations in shaping people’s lives.
Sample C3 Indicator Connections
Students will be able to explain the philosophical and historical foundations of the American political system. Students will be able to explain how civic engagement, legal obligations, and constitutional protections shape participation in the political process and influence democratic institutions.
Continue Students will be able to explain how the values and principles of the Constitution influence American institutions and practices and continue to shape the structure, functions, and practices of American political and legal institutions. Students will be able to explain the leadership structure of the state and national governments and the function in each branch of government.
Civic Virtues and Democratic Principles
D2.Civ.7.6-8. Apply civic virtues and democratic principles in school and community settings.
D2.Civ.8.6-8. Analyze ideas and principles contained in the founding documents of the United States, and explain how they influence the social and political system.
D2.Civ.10.6-8. Explain the relevance of personal interests and perspectives, civic virtues, and democratic principles when people address issues and problems in government and civil society.
Students will be able to explain the balance between common good and individual freedom. Students will be able to identify current and past local, state, national decisions that may conform or conflict with democratic ideals.
Rules, Processes, and Laws
D2.Civ.11.6-8. Differentiate among procedures for making decisions in the classroom, school, civil society, and local, state, and national government in terms of how civic purposes are intended.
D2.Civ.12.6-8. Assess specific rules and laws (both actual and proposed) as means of addressing public problems.
D2.Civ.13.6-8. Analyze the purposes, implementation, and consequences of public policies in multiple settings.
D2.Civ.14.6-8. Compare historical and contemporary means of changing societies, and promoting the common good.
Students will be able to examine how conflicts are resolved peacefully in a democratic society. Students will be able to participate in appropriate citizen actions to address community issue(s).
Economics
Theme: Economics and Policy
Exchange and Markets
D2.Eco.8.6-8. Explain how external benefits and costs influence market outcomes.
Students will be able to explain how government agencies protect investors and regulate financial markets and products.
The National Economy
D2.Eco.10.6-8. Explain the influence of changes in interest rates on borrowing and investing.
D2.Eco.11.6-8. Use appropriate data to evaluate the state of employment, unemployment, inflation, total production, income, and economic growth in the economy.
D2.Eco.12.6-8. Explain how inflation, deflation, and unemployment affect different groups.
Students will be able to explain the economic impact(s) of public policy decisions. Students will be able to explain the factors influencing GDP and how it reflects economic health.
The Global Economy
D2.Eco.14.6-8. Explain barriers to trade and how those barriers influence trade among nations.
D2.Eco.15.6-8. Explain the benefits and the costs of trade policies to individuals, businesses, and society.
Students will be able to explain the roles of global economic organizations in shaping economic policies and promoting international economic cooperation. Students will be able to explain the role of central banks and global economic factors in determining exchange rates. Students will be able to explain the concept of international debt and its implications for both developed and developing countries.
Continue Students will be able to identify evolving relationships between the United States and other nation-states around the world.
History
Theme: Civic Participation in a Democracy
Change, Continuity, and Context
D2.His.1.6-8. Analyze connections among events and developments in broader historical contexts.
D2.His.2.6-8. Classify series of historical events and developments as examples of change and/or continuity.
D2.His.3.6-8. Use questions generated about individuals and groups to analyze why they, and the developments they shaped, are seen as historically significant.
Students will be able to explain how the Constitution provides both continuity and flexibility in American government. Students will be able to compare how local, state, and national governments have changed in response to civic action.
Students will be able to explain how the rights of different groups of people have been limited or expanded throughout history.
Perspectives
D2.His.4.6-8. Analyze multiple factors that influenced the perspectives of people during different historical eras.
D2.His.5.6-8. Explain how and why perspectives of people have changed over time.
Students will be able to explain how social movements and events have changed attitudes about justice and equality.
Continue Students will be able to explain how Vermont’s views on issues have evolved.
Causation and Argumentation
D2.His.16.6-8. Organize applicable evidence into a coherent argument about the past.
D2.His.17.6-8. Compare the central arguments in secondary works of history on related topics in multiple media.
Students will be able to examine varied evidence from multiple sources and perspectives to develop an argument/solution to the issue.
